{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789225795,"nonce":"LoluqdO41GVliFJYf57IARroK64=","title":"A counted loop with a negative step never runs, and re-evaluates its bound each iteration","message":"`TypedCfgBuilder` lowers `for i in range(start, end, step)` to\n`i = start; while i \u003c end { body; i = i + step }`. The `\u003c` is fixed, so\n`range(5, 0, -1)` is false on entry and the body never runs; a positive\nstep that overshoots is fine, a negative one is silently empty. The\n`..`/`..=` range spelling has no step and is unaffected.\n\nThe `end` expression is also re-read in the header every iteration, so\n`for i in range(f())` calls `f` once per iteration where Python and\nevery range-loop language call it once.\n\nFix: bind `end` and `step` to hidden locals before the loop; pick the\ncomparison from the step's sign, statically when it is a literal and\nwith a runtime sign test otherwise.","files":null}]}